1
00:00:00,512 --> 00:00:03,446
Now we also have support for a sandbox. EVE

2
00:00:03,574 --> 00:00:05,482
supports that out of the box, and you can

3
00:00:05,482 --> 00:00:08,608
even configure those sandboxes. You can, for

4
00:00:08,608 --> 00:00:10,708
example, control the network policy to

5
00:00:10,756 --> 00:00:13,465
control which network requests can be made

6
00:00:13,577 --> 00:00:17,168
by the agent in its sandbox to, for example,

7
00:00:17,328 --> 00:00:20,534
block all network requests so that the agent

8
00:00:20,534 --> 00:00:22,538
can't reach out to the World Wide Web

9
00:00:22,698 --> 00:00:24,125
because this customer agent here, for

10
00:00:24,141 --> 00:00:26,289
example, doesn't need to. There is no reason

11
00:00:26,321 --> 00:00:28,164
for it to do web search or anything like

12
00:00:28,164 --> 00:00:29,816
that. And that can of course help with

13
00:00:29,848 --> 00:00:31,980
combating problems like data

14
00:00:50,223 --> 00:00:52,827
Xvolve VPS, for example, you would get

15
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:55,459
Docker as a default. And again, you don't

16
00:00:55,459 --> 00:00:57,198
need to do anything there. It'll spin up a

17
00:00:57,198 --> 00:00:59,539
Docker container and run those tool

18
00:00:59,539 --> 00:01:01,067
executions in that container automatically

19
00:01:01,132 --> 00:01:03,765
for you. That is that convenience you get

20
00:01:04,216 --> 00:01:06,782
from using a framework. Less control, but

21
00:01:06,782 --> 00:01:09,444
also less work for you to do. Now for that

22
00:01:09,444 --> 00:01:11,689
sandbox, you can also set up a workspace and

23
00:01:11,689 --> 00:01:14,142
for example, pre-populate that with some

24
00:01:14,238 --> 00:01:17,253
files the agent should have at its disposal.

25
00:01:17,686 --> 00:01:19,931
Here are some made-up knowledge base

26
00:01:19,931 --> 00:01:22,304
documents, for example, which the agent can

27
00:01:22,416 --> 00:01:24,501
analyze to help with customer support

28
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:27,355
queries. You could actually also turn some

29
00:01:27,387 --> 00:01:30,498
of them into skills because Eve does support

30
00:01:30,546 --> 00:01:31,059
skills.

31
00:01:31,590 --> 00:01:34,134
So you could add a skills folder to the

32
00:01:34,214 --> 00:01:36,066
agent folder and set up some skills there,

33
00:01:36,146 --> 00:01:38,465
which the agent can then activate just as

34
00:01:38,561 --> 00:01:40,783
you learned it. But here for this agent, I

35
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:43,858
did decide to not use skills and instead

36
00:01:43,971 --> 00:01:46,031
just set up such a general knowledge base

37
00:01:46,128 --> 00:01:46,257
here.

38
00:01:47,416 --> 00:01:49,332
But there is no wrong or right solution.

39
00:01:49,509 --> 00:01:51,059
When you're building this, you can simply

40
00:01:51,124 --> 00:01:53,258
try out different approaches and go with

41
00:01:53,323 --> 00:01:54,349
whatever you prefer.

42
00:01:55,812 --> 00:01:57,818
One other interesting feature about Eve,

43
00:01:57,834 --> 00:01:59,937
which we'll also see in action soon, is the

44
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:00,779
concept of channels, which basically

45
00:02:00,779 --> 00:02:01,297
controls how that agent

46
00:02:03,312 --> 00:02:06,845
can be invoked or used. Because since, as I

47
00:02:06,845 --> 00:02:08,338
mentioned, you'll often be building

48
00:02:08,434 --> 00:02:09,815
task-specific agents,

49
00:02:10,441 --> 00:02:13,524
you may not primarily be running this on the

50
00:02:13,524 --> 00:02:16,366
machine of you or some employee, but instead

51
00:02:16,463 --> 00:02:18,004
maybe on some shared VPS.

52
00:02:18,566 --> 00:02:20,236
So you can set up different channels like,

53
00:02:20,252 --> 00:02:22,693
for example, the Slack channel so that you

54
00:02:22,741 --> 00:02:25,213
can invoke this agent from inside Slack. And

55
00:02:25,246 --> 00:02:27,542
I'll go through the setup required for that

56
00:02:27,542 --> 00:02:28,377
in just a second.

57
00:02:29,162 --> 00:02:31,327
The Eve channel is the default, which sets

58
00:02:31,327 --> 00:02:34,260
up a little web server that can run locally

59
00:02:34,292 --> 00:02:36,616
on your system so that you can play around

60
00:02:36,616 --> 00:02:38,700
with it locally, or that could also be

61
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:41,105
deployed and you could put it behind your

62
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:43,253
website and wire up your own chat.

63
00:02:43,814 --> 00:02:45,305
The sky is the limit here.

64
00:02:45,834 --> 00:02:48,078
It's really up to your imagination and your

65
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:50,354
requirements. There also are many other

66
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:52,214
built-in channels you could use, and you can

67
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:54,731
also build your custom channels if you have

68
00:02:54,731 --> 00:02:56,269
your own channel that should be able to

69
00:02:56,318 --> 00:02:58,738
connect to this agent. But again, I won't

70
00:02:58,738 --> 00:03:00,839
turn this into a complete Eve course. This

71
00:03:00,839 --> 00:03:02,749
is just something to be aware of. You also

72
00:03:02,749 --> 00:03:05,239
see there are more features. You can, for

73
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:08,097
example, also register subagents. You can

74
00:03:08,193 --> 00:03:08,996
run your EVE

75
00:03:09,719 --> 00:03:13,027
agent on a schedule as an extra trigger, so

76
00:03:13,027 --> 00:03:15,741
to say, so that it runs every day at 9:00

77
00:03:15,773 --> 00:03:15,837
AM.

78
00:03:16,399 --> 00:03:18,936
You can set up evals, which is basically

79
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:22,212
testing for your agent where you let an AI

80
00:03:22,565 --> 00:03:25,408
use your agent and evaluate the results to

81
00:03:25,408 --> 00:03:27,014
see if the agent does what it

82
00:03:27,544 --> 00:03:27,945
should do.

83
00:03:28,506 --> 00:03:29,099
And more.

84
00:03:29,869 --> 00:03:32,642
But this agent already is a good example for

85
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:35,784
a demo basic customer support agent you may

86
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,605
want for your business, obviously then wired

87
00:03:38,605 --> 00:03:41,009
up to a proper database and with a proper

88
00:03:41,009 --> 00:03:41,698
knowledge base.

89
00:03:42,307 --> 00:03:44,471
But in general, it does have everything it

90
00:03:44,471 --> 00:03:47,741
needs for performing basic customer support

91
00:03:47,741 --> 00:03:48,206
tasks.

92
00:03:48,799 --> 00:03:50,963
So how can we now use it?
